<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 4 – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> I Like School!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like Games!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: productive: Write simple words with model guidance. interactive: Engage in interactive labeling activities.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple words related to school preferences with model guidance (e.g., writing 'games' or 'math' by copying from a model or example). Can copy and trace letters related to school activities (e.g., tracing 'P' for 'painting,' 'A' for 'Art'). Can draw and label favorite school activities collaboratively (e.g., drawing a math book and labeling it 'math').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, plays a short "What do you like?" song about school activities. The teacher shows flashcards of 'games,' 'math,' 'art,' 'music,' and 'playground.' Students repeat the words and use TPR (e.g., pretending to play games, count for math). The teacher asks, "Do you like games?" and encourages choral response "Yes, I like games!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a large flashcard with the word "GAMES" written clearly. The teacher points to each letter while saying its sound /g/, /a/, /m/, /e/, /s/ and then blends them to say "games," emphasizing the /g/ sound. The teacher then writes "I like games." on the board, pointing to each word as they say it, modeling the sentence structure. The teacher explicitly shows how to write each letter clearly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a game?" (pointing to a picture of 'games') – Expected answer: "Yes."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What sound does 'games' start with?" (Teacher points to 'G') – Expected answer: "/g/."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I Like School!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large chart titled "My Favorite School Activities" with simple drawings of a playground, an art easel, a math book, and a music note. Each drawing has the corresponding word ('playground', 'art', 'math', 'music') written clearly below it in large, traceable letters. Also, big flashcards with the target vocabulary words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher displays the "My Favorite School Activities" chart. The teacher points to each picture and its written word, saying, "Look! This is 'playground.' I like 'playground.' Do you like 'playground'?" The teacher elicits "Yes, I like playground!" or "No, I don't like playground." The teacher repeats this for 'art', 'math', and 'music,' focusing on the /l/ sound in "like" and the initial sounds of 'math' and 'games'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing likes and dislikes using simple phrases like 'I like...' or 'I don't like...' to express preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher calls out a word (e.g., "Math!") and students point to the correct picture and its word on the chart.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher holds up a flashcard (e.g., 'art') and students say the word aloud.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing vocabulary words related to school activities and simple 'I like...' sentences. Grammar: Subject + verb ('I like school.'), Present simple ('I like games.'). Vocabulary: 'games', 'math', 'art', 'music', 'playground'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Tracing & Copying Words:</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with dotted outlines of the target words: 'GAMES', 'MATH', 'ART', 'MUSIC', 'PLAYGROUND'. Next to each dotted word, there's a space for students to copy the word. The teacher models tracing each letter slowly and carefully on the board with a large stencil. Students trace the words, then copy them from the model. The teacher circulates, providing individual guidance and positive feedback on letter formation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Sentence Completion (Guided Writing):</b> The teacher provides another worksheet with incomplete sentences like "I like _______." below pictures of school activities (e.g., a picture of children playing games, a picture of a math book). Under each picture, there's a word bank containing 'games', 'math', 'art', 'music'. Students look at the picture and the word bank, then copy the correct word into the blank to complete the sentence "I like [activity]." The teacher ensures students understand to copy the word and not just trace.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Labeling Check:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a large piece of paper with a simple drawing of a school scene (e.g., a classroom with different activity corners). They are also given word cards with 'games', 'math', 'art', 'music', 'playground'. Together, they decide where each activity is happening in the drawing and collaboratively glue the word card next to the correct picture. They then verbally say "I like [word]" for each labeled item. The teacher walks around, listening and prompting with "Do you like it?" and "Yes/No."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I Like School!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual creative drawing and guided labeling (written product).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will create a "My Favorite Activity" poster. Each student receives a blank sheet of paper. The teacher instructs them to draw their favorite school activity from the vocabulary learned (e.g., drawing themselves playing games, counting in math, painting in art). After drawing, they will look at a word bank displayed on the board (containing 'games', 'math', 'art', 'music', 'playground') and copy the word that matches their drawing onto a provided line below their artwork. The teacher provides an example: "I like art" written under a drawing of a child painting. They are encouraged to try writing "I like" before the word if they feel comfortable, using a model provided by the teacher.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students can then briefly show their "My Favorite Activity" poster to a classmate and say, "I like [their drawn activity]," reinforcing the pragmatic function of expressing preferences and sharing sociolinguistically.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer review.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' "My Favorite Activity" posters from Stage 4. The teacher collects the posters.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can copy simple words with model guidance (e.g., 'games', 'math').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Drawing matches the labeled activity.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to write "I like" (if applicable).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a small paper with two faces: a happy face and a neutral face. They draw a circle around the face that shows how they feel about writing words today. They can also draw a small picture of their favorite written word next to the face.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word was easy for you to write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new thing you learned about writing?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., the tracing activities, the visual models, collaborative labeling).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next writing lesson (e.g., more practice with specific letters, clearer spacing guidelines, more individual teacher support during copying)?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration), students will use their writing skills to collaboratively create a class "Our Favorite School Activities" book, integrating their drawings and labels, and sharing them with another class or parents.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>